course: dictionary
lesson: 1354
title: Candidates and context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A candidate is a person who is trying to be chosen, or a person taking an exam. When you meet a word with more than one meaning, the words around it show you which meaning to use. «Before an election, candidates try to get noticed» tells you that candidates are people trying to win an election.
explain: Look at the exam sense: «Candidates must not talk.» The clue is the word exam or test near it. Swap in your meaning: "Pupils taking the exam must not talk" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Candidate can also mean someone likely to be chosen. «She is a strong candidate for captain» means she has a good chance of getting the job. The phrase "candidate for" is a good clue. Always ask: what meaning fits these words?
fact: A candidate is a person trying to be chosen for something, such as a job, a prize or a place in government.
fact: A candidate is also a person taking an exam.
fact: «Negative campaigning» is when a candidate criticises the other candidate instead of praising their own.
fact: In Australia, voters number the candidates in order of preference.
fact: Useful phrases: candidate for, a strong candidate, election candidate and exam candidate.
exercise: «Before an election, candidates try to get noticed with adverts, slogans and posters.» Who are the candidates? | *People trying to be chosen in the election | The posters that cover the lawns | The people who count the votes | The rallies and the cheering crowds
exercise: Ella says, «She is a strong candidate for captain.» What does candidate mean here? | A person who loses every game | A ticket to sports day | A pupil writing an exam | *Someone likely to be chosen
exercise: «An attack ad criticises the other candidate instead of praising your own.» Which word means the opposite of criticises? | *Praising | Own | Attack | Candidate
test: «Candidates must not talk.» In an exam, who are the candidates? | Parents who wait outside | People who stand in an election | Teachers who mark the papers | *Pupils taking the exam
test: «Running is like a mini election: you are a candidate, you give a short speech, and the best promises are ones you can really try to keep.» Which promise is the best for a class candidate? | *Asking for more football time | Shouting so everyone hears | Free sweets every Friday | Promising to be the teacher
exam: Which sentence uses candidate to mean a person likely to be chosen? | Each candidate writes a long manifesto | *She is a strong candidate for captain | The voters count the posters | Candidates must sit in silence
